{"name":"Sage Wisdom","hex":"#afad96","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sage","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Wise.","Grave; serious; solemn."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very wise person or spiritual teacher; someone of gravity and wisdom, especially, a teacher venerable for years, and of sound judgment and prudence; a grave or stoic philosopher."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sage"},{"term":"wisdom","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An element of personal character that enables one to distinguish the wise from the unwise.","A piece of wise advice."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wisdom"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sage (philosophy)","blocks":[{"text":"A sage, in classical philosophy, is someone who has attained wisdom. The term has also been used interchangeably with a 'good person', and a 'virtuous person'. Some of the earliest accounts of the sage begin with Empedocles' Sphairos. Horace describes the Sphairos as \"Completely within itself, well-rounded and spherical, so that nothing extraneous can adhere to it, because of its smooth and polished surface.\" Alternatively, the sage is one who lives \"according to an ideal which transcends the everyday.\""}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(philosophy)","wikidata":"Q2624172"},{"title":"Wisdom","blocks":[{"text":"Wisdom, also known as sapience, is defined by psychologists as a combination of \"wit and virtue,\" the ability to apply expert tacit and experiential knowledge, balancing intrapersonal, interpersonal and institutional interests, to deal with complex questions of life and attain a common good. Wisdom can be viewed as a stable personality trait, but also as context-bound process, meaning that a person in some contexts behaves wisely, but in other contexts does not. Wisdom can also be viewed as influenced and determined by sociocultural norms and traditions. Wisdom can be viewed solely as wise behaviour, describing actions which lead to \"an altruistic outcome by creatively and successfully solving problems.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Luca_Giordano_-_Dream_of_Solomon_-_WGA09004.jpg/330px-Luca_Giordano_-_Dream_of_Solomon_-_WGA09004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisdom","wikidata":"Q799"},{"title":"Sage (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Sage is a grey-green resembling that of dried sage leaves. As a quaternary color, it is an equal mix of the tertiary colors citron and slate. The hex RGB color value of the adjacent sage swatch is BCB88A. For decades, some military flight jackets were made in sage green."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b2/Sage_iPhone_17.jpg/330px-Sage_iPhone_17.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sage_(color)","wikidata":"Q7399114"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Center_detail%2C_from-_Interior_of_a_Book_Cover-_Manjuvajra_Embracing_His_Consort%2C_with_Attendant_Lamas_MET_DP335604_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Center_detail%2C_from-_Interior_of_a_Book_Cover-_Manjuvajra_Embracing_His_Consort%2C_with_Attendant_Lamas_MET_DP335604_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Center_detail%2C_from-_Interior_of_a_Book_Cover-_Manjuvajra_Embracing_His_Consort%2C_with_Attendant_Lamas_MET_DP335604_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Center_detail%2C_from-_Interior_of_a_Book_Cover-_Manjuvajra_Embracing_His_Consort%2C_with_Attendant_Lamas_MET_DP335604_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Tibetan book cover depicting Prajñāpāramitā Devi and Mañjuśrī in yab yum, late 13th century","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACenter_detail%2C_from-_I